WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
- Hidden amongst all the pain

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.150
and stress of studying for exams,

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.380
you might find that physics can actually

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.000
be a very beautiful subject.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.110
Whilst physics at school is
often about meeting standards,

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.050
pleasing examiners, or being competitive,

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.420
real physics and math beyond that

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.000
can be rewarding and interesting.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.120
I recently made some videos
about the very stressful

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.770
the university entrance exam from Turkey

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:30.020
and there was a fair amount
of despair in the comments.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.050
Today I wanted to show a positive example

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.140
of achievement coming out of Turkey

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.940
and I wanted to do that
through telling the story

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.190
of Feza Gursey who was a very successful

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.310
mathematician and physicist.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.940
I find him very inspiring because not only

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.500
was he working on one of the
hardest ideas in physics,

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.050
but he also relentlessly
pursued all knowledge

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.210
art, music, poetry, and beautiful ideas.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.080
He made significant contributions

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:02.050
to general relativity and particle physics

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.630
where he found symmetries

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.120
that he expressed using group theory.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.490
Some of his work would
inspire what would go on

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.027
to be called quantum chromodynamics

00:01:11.027 --> 00:01:13.760
and Gursey was highly respected by many

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.610
of the top physicists.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.760
To follow his journey,

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.440
let's start with a school report card.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.380
Gursey was born in Istanbul in April 1921,

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.420
but attended primary school in Paris.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.660
This report card is from January 1928

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.480
so when he was only six years old

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.130
but he was already earning
the respect of his teachers.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.420
His conduct and order are said to be good,

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:45.420
and for reading, writing,
calculation and spelling

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.780
there is place first
written in this column

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.080
so I guess he was top of his class.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.720
There's also a first for
grammar and for recitation.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.550
Overall, he's said to
have been doing very good.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.360
Later he would return to Turkey

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.240
and become a boarding student
at Galatasaray High School.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.790
This is one of the
enrollment documents there.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.980
It's got his name and the number 39

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:10.530
which was assigned to him,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.860
his father's name,

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.140
that he was born in Istanbul in 1921,

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.150
that he's a boarding student
who entered the school in 1930.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.630
This is stamped 1939
when he would have been

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.670
in the 11th grade

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.290
and here is a picture of him at that time.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:32.290
Here's a copy of phases
report card from 1939 to 1940,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.050
there's nothing too
scandalous to report here

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.800
in fact, it looks like he
was quite a good student

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.830
and fairly well rounded.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.090
Down this column we have the subjects,

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.970
and then I believe these are
grades from written exams,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.100
the first two columns,

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.060
and then an opinion note

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.420
and then the overall passing grades

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.440
signed off by the teachers.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.240
There's a lot of nines and tens here

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.490
which I believe to be the highest.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.110
The subjects here are literature, history,

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.930
geography, natural sciences, all tens,

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.090
also straight tens for
physics and chemistry

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.940
and there's foreign language,

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.630
philosophy, mechanics,
geometry, and algebra.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:14.280
The second page would detail any penalties

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:15.940
but he doesn't seem to have any

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.110
and this is the attendance.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:22.200
These are the rows for
days off and days late

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.980
and it's signed by the school directors

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.460
and indicates an overall pass.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.850
Gursey graduated from
the science department

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:32.645
of this high school in 1940
at the top of his class

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:35.710
and he was said to me, a legendary student

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:38.970
who impressed both local
and foreign teachers.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:41.230
It was in high school that he decided

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.100
to dedicate his life to physics.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.240
A classmate of Gursey said that

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.780
at school during evening study hours,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.010
you could often find Feza reading

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.817
Proust's philosophical stories named

00:03:53.817 --> 00:03:56.040
"In Search of Lost Time."

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.910
This is a long novel
that comes in seven parts

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.380
and it's said to try to find an answer

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.380
to some of life's
questions such as who am I?

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.850
And what am I to make of this life?

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:09.660
Feza was also said to often be examining

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.870
the artworks of Cezanne

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.330
and Feza's teachers would often call him

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.670
into the teacher lounge during
breaks to chat with him.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.260
After graduating high school,

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.660
Gursey attended the Istanbul University

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:27.660
and graduated with a degree
in physics and math in 1944

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.130
and he graduated top of his class.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.470
He found the curriculum
at the science department

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.130
to not be challenging enough

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:36.020
so he still had plenty of time

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:40.010
to nurture his interests
in poetry and art.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:41.457
However he said,

00:04:41.457 --> 00:04:44.760
"I have to learn physics
in spite of everything"

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.940
perhaps indicating that he thought

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.860
he could excel in many different fields,

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.030
but felt the strongest
calling towards physics.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.450
He went to Imperial College in London

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:59.590
for his PhD which he received in 1950

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:01.377
with his thesis titled,

00:05:01.377 --> 00:05:05.070
"Application of Quaternions
to Quantum Field."

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.525
While he was a grad student,

00:05:06.525 --> 00:05:08.840
Gursey wrote a letter to his mother

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.610
which seems to have come from a time

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.020
when he was struggling to make progress,

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.180
but was still holding
on to his motivations.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:19.067
He says, "I am not hanging out at parties

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:22.527
"as you assume dear mother,
I work day and night.

00:05:22.527 --> 00:05:24.577
"I have been holding on to the things

00:05:24.577 --> 00:05:26.957
"that I want to work on for two years

00:05:26.957 --> 00:05:29.927
"with all my strength:
general field theory

00:05:29.927 --> 00:05:33.107
"and relations between
electromagnetic meson

00:05:33.107 --> 00:05:35.027
"and electron fields.

00:05:35.027 --> 00:05:39.157
"What I am currently working
on cannot go beyond formalism,

00:05:39.157 --> 00:05:40.747
"but I hope that at least if

00:05:40.747 --> 00:05:43.857
"the Cambridge Philosophical
Society accepts me,

00:05:43.857 --> 00:05:45.607
"I can show my supervisor

00:05:45.607 --> 00:05:48.150
"that I am not wasting all my time."

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.850
He'd said that he was trying to get

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:54.010
fundamental equations
into quaternion formalism,

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:56.850
but that his supervisor
thought it was unnecessary

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.680
and priggish to focus on quaternions.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.130
But taking a look at his thesis
published three years later,

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.760
it does look like he
made some good progress.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.670
The abstract says that
the object of the thesis

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.500
is to show that the quaternion algebra

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.420
can be applied successfully
to the discussion

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:17.010
and solution of directs equation

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.410
and that it can be extended
to general relativity

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:24.200
to give an invariant
formulation of field equations.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.520
Much of his work after this
focused on group theory

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.260
and its application to particle physics.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:33.430
In reality, the work that
he did is very advanced

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.720
and takes a lot of prior knowledge

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.060
to understand where it's coming from.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.230
But let me give just the briefest

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.470
of introductions to group theory,

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:45.180
to kind of set the stage for the space

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:46.840
that he was working in.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.170
But first, I would like to
quickly thank Shortform.

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:51.610
Looking for ways to increase

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:53.390
how much I can learn from books,

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:55.050
I came across Shortform

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.070
who have kindly sponsored this video.

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:59.710
Shortform provides detailed book summaries

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:02.050
of the best nonfiction books.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:05.250
It's a way to take books
you've always wanted to read

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.130
and learn the key ideas to a deeper level.

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:10.250
I wouldn't otherwise have time to read

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:12.810
a lot of these books in their entirety.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:14.840
Their categories cover many topics

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:18.460
including philosophy,
science and communication.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:20.230
I think the books on communication

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:23.020
are the most useful for
my own personal growth,

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.070
like this one called "Quiet"

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:28.540
about how introverts can
embrace their personality

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.550
instead of trying to force themselves

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.950
to be extroverted to fit in.

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:36.110
Shortform drops new book
guides and articles every week,

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:40.060
by joining through my
link, shortform.com/tibees

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:42.675
you'll get five days of unlimited access

00:07:42.675 --> 00:07:47.050
and an additional 20% off
the annual subscription

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:47.980
so check it out

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:50.070
the link will be down in the description.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:51.900
Now back to the group theory.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.940
Here I have a square and a circle

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.130
and just looking at these two shapes,

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:59.700
the circle could be said
to be more symmetrical

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:01.050
because there are more ways

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.900
that you could rotate it for example,

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.810
and it would still look the same.

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:10.600
In physics a symmetry is a
feature that remains unchanged

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:12.870
under some transformation.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:15.743
If that transformation is rotation,

00:08:15.743 --> 00:08:20.320
then the circle has a lot
more symmetry than the square.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.980
There are an infinity of ways
that I could rotate the circle

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:28.010
and still be left with
what I started with.

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:31.140
However, for the square,
there are some rotations

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:34.570
that don't leave it
unchanged like this one here.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:39.410
In fact, only rotations of
90 degrees or multiples of 90

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:41.700
would be considered symmetrical.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:42.810
If we were to write down

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.980
the set of symmetries for the square,

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:49.980
we'd have 90, 180, 270 and 360

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.670
but one of these rotations is special

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:57.670
a rotation by 360 degrees

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:01.080
would have us ending up
back where we started

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:04.860
which is the same as a
rotation by zero degrees.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.300
We call this the identity element

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:09.520
since it does remain unchanged.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.410
Adding any of these rotations,

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:14.130
will give you another rotation in the set.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:18.490
For example, 90 + 180 = 270.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:22.110
Also, every rotation here can be undone.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:26.050
If you've done a rotation
of 270 and want to undo it,

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:29.190
you can do an additional rotation of 90

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:34.190
because 270 + 90 is 360
and that's the identity.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.810
The second rotation would be considered

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:38.960
the inverse of the first one

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:42.320
and an inverse exists
for every single element.

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:44.640
All these properties of these rotations

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.310
that I have mentioned
are actually properties

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:50.270
of what in maths is called a group

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:54.270
and this set of rotations is
just one example of a group.

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.380
The symmetries of the
circle are also a group

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.250
but this time the elements
of the group are infinite

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.170
and correspond to each
possible angle of rotation.

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.827
In particle physics there
is a group called SU(3)

00:10:07.827 --> 00:10:11.630
which is very similar to
the symmetries of a sphere

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:15.510
and physicists have used
representations of this group,

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:19.240
to come up with structures
of subatomic particles.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.370
There is a lot of symmetry that exists

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.160
within the standard model

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.090
and often these strange new particles

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:29.280
are predicted before we
find them in the lab,

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:31.520
because we can see there is a gap

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.030
or something missing in the symmetry.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:35.880
If you want to go further into this,

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:37.280
I'll link to you the ideas

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.850
of Lie groups down in the description,

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:42.440
but this will also show you the basic idea

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:45.100
which Feza Gursey was working in,

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.760
and the kinds of topics that
he was expanding with his work.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:52.180
Group theory is one of
the many areas in physics

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.657
where you can follow this quote,

00:10:54.657 --> 00:10:58.120
"Beauty is truth, truth beauty"

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:02.580
in the sense that pursuing
symmetries and what seems to work

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:06.530
to make the equations
simple and beautiful,

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:09.170
can lead you to what is true.

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:10.610
It isn't always the case

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:14.200
but often it seems to lead
you in the right direction.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:17.650
Some of Feza Gursey's most
important contributions

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:19.387
include this paper

00:11:19.387 --> 00:11:22.950
"On the Symmetries of Strong
and Weak Interactions"

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:24.960
published in 1960.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.930
He also proposed something
called SU(6) symmetry

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:31.160
for baryons and mesons.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:34.920
He also applied group theory
to scattering of particles

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.120
and to general relativity.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.151
After Gursey died in 1992,

00:11:40.151 --> 00:11:42.790
Ed Witten wrote about
him and said that he was

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:46.240
one of the most respected
members of the physics community.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:49.440
He said that Feza's
scientific work is marked

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.158
with remarkable originality and elegance

00:11:52.158 --> 00:11:54.690
as well as intellectual courage.

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:56.870
He never hesitated to pick problems

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:58.330
that were not fashionable.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:01.400
He worked at them in depth, planting seeds

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.380
that in some cases developed

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:05.730
into whole branches of our discipline.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.180
He continues that, subsequent attempts

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:12.580
to understand Feza's work
on the SU(6) symmetry

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:14.970
led to the introduction
of the color of degrees

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:17.030
of freedom of quarks.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:21.590
Ed Witten continued to describe
Feza as a very cultured man.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:24.500
One that you could have
very deep discussions with

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:29.500
on the topics of music,
poetry, novels and paintings

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:35.050
in short, on essentially any
subject of depth and beauty.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.680
I hadn't known about Feza
Gursey until recently

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.160
and so I hope sharing his story helps

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.680
a few more people to know about him,

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.740
but also to provide a bit of motivation

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:51.110
for Turkish students especially
studying for tough exams.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:54.140
But I also find his story to be a reminder

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.240
to stay well rounded

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.730
physics after all, is
about the world around us

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:04.550
and understanding it not
just about passing exams.

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:05.740
Thanks Shortform

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:08.010
and thank you for watching this video.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:11.900
A special shout out to today's
Patron Cats of the Day,

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:12.985
Leo and Mischa.

00:13:12.985 --> 00:13:15.568
(gentle music)

